'''Walex Cantwell Blissex I''' was a legendary [[human]] [[starship]] designer who served as an [[Alliance officer|officer]] in the [[Alliance to Restore the Republic]]. Blissex's work included designing the [[Delta-7 Aethersprite-class light interceptor|Delta-7 ''Aethersprite''-class light interceptor]], the [[Alpha-3 Nimbus-class V-wing starfighter|Alpha-3 ''Nimbus''-class V-wing starfighter]], the [[RZ-1 A-wing interceptor]], the ''[[Gladiator I]]'' [[Star Destroyer]], and other powerful [[destroyer]]s of the [[Galactic Republic]]. The [[Galactic Empire]]'s [[Cantwell-class Arrestor Cruiser|''Cantwell''-class Arrestor Cruiser]] was named after Blissex.

Blissex had a daughter, [[Lira Wessex]], who was a ship designer in her own right. Prior to the [[Battle of Endor]] in [[4 ABY]], Blissex joined members of [[Alliance High Command]] and other personnel aboard the Alliance [[flagship]] ''[[Home One]]'' for the briefing detailing their plans to destroy the Empire's [[DS-2 Death Star II Mobile Battle Station]], a [[planet]]-destroying [[superweapon]].

==Biography==
===Early works===
[[File:V-wing_BF2.png|thumb|left|265px|Walex Blissex designed the V-wing starfighter (pictured), which was deployed by 19 BBY.]]
Walex Cantwell Blissex I<ref name="Dawn">''[[Star Wars: Dawn of Rebellion: The Visual Guide]]''</ref> operated in the [[Republic Era]], during which he<ref name="BMF 58" /> designed the [[Delta-7 Aethersprite-class light interceptor|Delta-7 ''Aethersprite''-class light interceptor]],<ref name="BMF 25">{{BuildFalconCite|25|Starship Fact File}}</ref> which entered service by [[42 BBY]].<ref name="Delta-7 note">{{SW|url=news/marvel-mace-windu-1-preview|text=Marvel's ''Mace Windu'' #1 Showcases the Prequel Hero in an Early Adventure – Exclusive Preview}} establishes the events of [[Mace Windu 1|''Mace Windu'' 1]], which depict a [[Delta-7 Aethersprite-class light interceptor|Delta-7 ''Aethersprite''-class light interceptor]], takes place prior to [[Mace Windu]]'s entry into the [[Jedi High Council]], which is depicted in "[[Choices]]." As the episode occurs prior to [[Dooku]]'s departure from the [[Jedi Order]], which ''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates to [[42 BBY]], ''Mace Windu'' 1 must take place by that [[Standard year|year]], and the Delta-7 must also have been in use by the same year.</ref> The renowned Blissex went on to design another model<ref name="BMF 25" /> of the [[Delta-series fighter|Delta-series]];<ref name="Collapse">''[[Collapse of the Republic]]''</ref> the [[Alpha-3 Nimbus-class V-wing starfighter|Alpha-3 ''Nimbus''-class V-wing starfighter]].<ref name="BMF 25" /> The latter was used by the [[Galactic Republic]]'s [[Republic Starfighter Corps|starfighter forces]] during the [[Clone Wars]],<ref name="Episode III">{{Film|III}}</ref> seeing use by [[19 BBY]].<ref name="V-wing note">{{FFGXW2|set=Siege of Coruscant Scenario Pack|cardname="Contrail"}} depicts an [[Alpha-3 Nimbus-class V-wing starfighter|Alpha-3 ''Nimbus''-class V-wing starfighter]] during the [[Battle of Coruscant]], which ''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates to [[19 BBY]]. Therefore, the V-wing must have been in service by that [[time]].</ref>

When designing the V-wing, Blissex used his knowledge of the special modifications that [[Jedi General]]s [[Plo Koon]], [[Saesee Tiin]], and [[Anakin Skywalker]] made to [[Blade of Dorin|their]] [[Saesee Tiin's Delta-7B Aethersprite-class light interceptor|own]] [[Anakin Skywalker's Delta-7B Aethersprite-class light interceptor|Delta-7]] [[starfighter]]s and complemented them with his own new ideas. The designer understood that Delta-7s flown by the [[Jedi]] frequently left their [[V-19 Torrent starfighter]] [[squadron]]s behind due to their advanced speed. As such, he sought to resolve the issue and oversaw the creation of contemporary [[computer]] flight systems based on complicated tactical flight routines installed in V-wings. This modification allowed the [[Clone trooper pilot|clone pilots]] flying the V-wings to finally keep up with their Jedi leaders.<ref name="BMF 58" /> The V-wing had other variants, including the [[Alpha-3B "Besh"]]<ref name="V-wing B">{{FFGXW2|set=Nimbus-class V-Wing Expansion Pack|cardname=Alpha-3B "Besh"}}</ref> and [[Alpha-3E "Esk"]].<ref name="V-wing E">{{FFGXW2|set=Nimbus-class V-Wing Expansion Pack|cardname=Alpha-3E "Esk"}}</ref> Additionally, Blissex designed and conceived the ''[[Gladiator I]]'' [[Star Destroyer]] as a Star Destroyer escort to be used in heavy battle lines during the Clone Wars,<ref name="Friends Like These">''[[Friends Like These]]''</ref> and to serve in multiple roles such as [[Orbital bombardment|bombardment]], [[Patrol ship|patrol]], siege, and attack ships. Blissex also designed other powerful [[destroyer]]s of the Republic.<ref name="Ships">{{EncyclopediaCite|Starships of the Empire and the Rebellion}}</ref> He eventually had a daughter, [[Lira Wessex]], who became a [[Starship|ship]] designer herself.<ref name="BMF 35" />

===Imperial Era===
[[File:General_Walex_Blissex_RaR.png|thumb|right|250px|Alliance officer Walex Blissex, looking over the A-wing's design]]
With the Republic's [[Proclamation of the New Order|transformation]] into the [[Galactic Empire]], Blissex's V-wing design continued to see service in the [[Imperial Military]].<ref name="Episode III" /> The foundation for the Empire's new [[TIE/ln space superiority starfighter]] could be traced to Blissex's V-wing and other Republic craft.<ref name="Rogue One Guide">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: The Ultimate Visual Guide]]''</ref> The Empire's [[Cantwell-class Arrestor Cruiser|''Cantwell''-class Arrestor Cruiser]],<ref name="Dawn" /> which was active by [[13 BBY]],<ref name="Solo date">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates the prologue of ''[[Solo: A Star Wars Story]]'', which features the [[Cantwell-class Arrestor Cruiser|''Cantwell''-class Arrestor Cruiser]], to [[13 BBY]].</ref> was named for Blissex.<ref name="Dawn" /> Blissex also designed the [[RZ-1 A-wing interceptor]],<ref name="BMF 25" /> a starfighter based on the [[R-22 Spearhead]]<ref name="TLJ ICS">''[[Star Wars: The Last Jedi: Incredible Cross-Sections]]''</ref> which was rejected by the Empire but used by the rebel [[Phoenix Cell]]<ref name="Dawn" /> by [[4 BBY]].<ref name="Rebels Date">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates the events of ''[[Star Wars Rebels: The Siege of Lothal]]'', in which the [[RZ-1 A-wing interceptor]] appears, to [[4 BBY]].</ref> The RZ-1 was pressed into wider service with the [[Alliance to Restore the Republic]].<ref name="Rebel Files">''[[Star Wars: The Rebel Files]]''</ref> Although it did not witness action during the Clone Wars, Blissex's ''Gladiator I'' design was also used by the Empire, which had repurposed its role to that of a patrol craft, with it in use by the [[time]]<ref name="Friends Like These" /> between [[2 BBY]] and [[4 ABY]].<ref name="Date Note">''[[Friends Like These]]'' establishes that the ''[[Gladiator I]]'' [[Star Destroyer]] ''[[Blood Ambition]]'' participated in battle against the [[Alliance to Restore the Republic]] during the [[Battle of Xorrn]]. According to ''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'', the Alliance was established in [[2 BBY]] and reorganized into the [[New Republic]] in [[4 ABY]]. Therefore, the ''Gladiator I'' must have been active by the [[time]] between 2 [['ABY-BBY' dating system|BBY]] and 4 ABY.</ref>

By 4 [['ABY-BBY' dating system|ABY]],<ref name="Timelines">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates the events of {{Film|VI}} to [[4 ABY]].</ref> during the [[Galactic Civil War]], Blissex served as an [[Alliance officer|officer]] of the Rebel Alliance and joined members of [[Alliance High Command]] and other personnel aboard<ref name="Episode VI" /> the Alliance [[flagship]]<ref name="Ships" /> ''[[Home One]]'' to set forth the Alliance plan of attack against the [[DS-2 Death Star II Mobile Battle Station]], a [[planet]]-destroying [[superweapon]]. Blissex spoke with several Alliance personnel prior to the briefing and stood behind<ref name="Episode VI" /> [[Chancellor (Rebel Alliance)|Chief of State]]<ref name="Rogue One Guide" /> [[Mon Mothma]], [[Admiral]] [[Gial Ackbar]], and [[General]] [[Crix Madine]] as they delivered their presentation. The Rebel Alliance emerged victorious at the subsequent [[Battle of Endor]].<ref name="Episode VI" /> At one point as an Alliance [[officer]], Blissex observed the designs of the RZ-1.<ref name="Blissex">{{Armada|set=Upgrade Card Collection|cardname=Walex Blissex}}</ref> In contrast, Blissex's daughter, Lira,<ref name="BMF 25" /> had served the Galactic Empire.<ref name="Armada">{{Armada|set=Super Star Destroyer Expansion Pack|cardname=Lira Wessex}}</ref> Blissex and Wessex shared their names with the [["Blissex-head" bit-driver|"Blissex-head"]] and [["Wessex-head" bit-driver]]s, respectively.<ref name="TFA VD">''[[Star Wars: The Force Awakens: The Visual Dictionary]]''</ref>

==Personality and traits==
Walex Blissex was a [[human]] with [[Color|fair]] skin, dark [[eye]]s, and graying fair [[hair]].<ref name="Episode VI" />

==Skills and abilities==
Blissex was a highly renowned [[engineer]]<ref name="BMF 35" /> and starship designer, capable of recognizing design issues with starfighters and overseeing complex computer technology. He was knowledgeable in designing starfighter craft<ref name="BMF 58" /> as well as larger ships such as the ''Gladiator I'' Star Destroyer.<ref name="Friends Like These" />

==Equipment==
Blissex donned an [[Alliance military uniform|Alliance officer military uniform]], which included a blue collared [[Clothing|shirt]] with pins, worn under a beige military tunic. He also wore a brown [[utility belt]] and black [[boot]]s.<ref name="Episode VI" />

==Behind the scenes==
[[File:Madine_Endor_Briefing.png|thumb|280px|left|The character of Walex Blissex (far right) first appeared in the rebel briefing scene in ''Star Wars'': Episode VI ''Return of the Jedi'', which was released in 1983.]]
Walex Cantwell Blissex I<ref name="Dawn" /> debuted in the final film of the [[original trilogy]], [[1983]]'s [[Star Wars: Episode VI Return of the Jedi|''Star Wars'': Episode VI ''Return of the Jedi'']], portrayed by an uncredited actor.<ref name="Episode VI" /> Blissex was subsequently identified in the current ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[canon]] in the [[Star Wars: Build the Millennium Falcon 25|twenty-fifth issue]] of [[De Agostini]]'s ''[[Star Wars: Build the Millennium Falcon]]'' magazine,<ref name="BMF 25" /> which was released around [[June 24]], [[2015]].<ref name="BMFdate">{{BMFdates|25}}</ref>  The [[2023]] reference book ''[[Star Wars: Dawn of Rebellion: The Visual Guide]]'',<ref name="Dawn" /> in a section written by [[Pablo Hidalgo]],<ref name="DawnAuthor">{{WookYouTube|-nqSqVOvfM4|Wookieepedia Interviews - Emily Shkoukani & Jason Fry}}</ref> further expanded Blissex's name to Walex Cantwell Blissex I,<ref name="Dawn" /> sharing the name of famed ''[[Star Wars]]'' concept artist [[Colin Cantwell]]. The ''Cantwell''-class Arrestor Cruiser was named after Colin Cantwell,<ref name="Daughters of Ferrix">{{SW|url=news/andor-episode-11-daughter-of-ferrix-highlights|text=''Andor'' Analyzed: 5 Highlights from Episode 11, "Daughter of Ferrix"}}</ref> which, [[in-universe]], is named after Blissex.<ref name="Dawn" />

Blissex originated in the [[Star Wars Legends|''Star Wars'' Legends]] continuity, where he was first mentioned in ''[[The Star Wars Sourcebook]]'' authored by [[Bill Slavicsek]] and [[Curtis Smith]] and published in [[1987]] by [[West End Games]] for use with ''[[Star Wars: The Roleplaying Game]]''.<ref name="SW Sourcebook">''[[The Star Wars Sourcebook]]''</ref> The [[2000]] ''[[Death Star II Limited]]'' set in the [[Star Wars Customizable Card Game|''Star Wars'' Customizable Card Game]] later [[Retroactive continuity|retconned]] Blissex as one of the [[Officer/Legends|officers]] appearing in ''Return of the Jedi''.<ref name="SWCCG">{{CCG|set=Death Star II Limited|cardname=General Walex Blissex}}</ref>

[[File:HaashnBTS.jpg|thumb|right|280px|Filming of the rebel briefing scene, where Blissex appeared in, took place in February 1982.]]
The shooting of scene fifty-four&mdash;the large rebel briefing in which Blissex's character appeared&mdash;was filmed on the INT. HEADQUARTERS &mdash; MAIN BRIEFING ROOM set located on Stage 5 at [[Elstree Studios]] between Friday, [[February 12]] and Monday, [[February 15]], [[1982]]. The scene included the principal characters, including Admiral Ackbar, Mon Mothma, and General Madine, and the call sheet listed seventy-three other characters. Among these, seven "Rebel Generals" were included. For the scene's filming, extras that were already established as Imperials were given masks to wear, and the extras in the filming of scene fifty-four were mostly actors who [[David Tomblin]], first assistant director, had previously relied on.<ref name="Making of ROTJ">''[[The Making of Star Wars: Return of the Jedi]]''</ref>

